<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Afrasiab</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Afrasiyab</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Afrasiab is the archaeological site of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, a large mound on the northern edge of the modern city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Inhabited from about the 7th century BCE until the Mongol destruction of 1220, it was the Sogdian capital known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Maracanda. Its best-known find is a palace hall with 7th-century murals, the Ambassadors' Painting, made for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varkhuman">Varkhuman</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/afrasiab.jpg" alt="Afrasiab" width="198" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Afrasiyab_(The_Shahnama_of_Shah_Tahmasp).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
